[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Test-Driven Development (TDD) in JavaScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Test-Driven Development (TDD) is a software development process that relies on the repetition of a very short development cycle: first the developer writes an (initially failing) automated test case that defines a desired improvement or new function, then produces the minimum amount of code to pass that test, and finally refactors the new code to acceptable standards. This tutorial will guide you through implementing TDD in JavaScript."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Basic understanding of JavaScript."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Familiarity with a JavaScript testing framework (e.g., Jest, Mocha, Jasmine)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Node.js and npm (Node Package Manager) installed."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up the Environment"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, create a new directory for your project and initialize a Node.js project:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"mkdir tdd-example
cd tdd-example
npm init -y"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, install a testing framework. We'll use Jest in this example:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"npm install --save-dev jest"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Add a test script to your `package.json` file:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"{ 
  "name": "tdd-example",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "",
  "main": "index.js",
  "scripts": {
    "test": "jest"
  },
  "keywords": [],
  "author": "",
  "license": "ISC",
  "devDependencies": {
    "jest": "^29.0.0"
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing Your First Test"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's create a simple function that adds two numbers. Following TDD, we'll write the test first. Create a file named `sum.test.js`:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// sum.test.js
const sum = require('./sum');

describe('sum', () => {
  it('should add two numbers correctly', () => {
    expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3);
  });
});"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing the Function"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, create a file named `sum.js` with the minimum code to pass the test:"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"// sum.js
function sum(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

module.exports = sum;"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Running the Test"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the test using the command:"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"npm test"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"You should see the test pass. If it fails, review your code and test case."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactoring"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this simple example, there's not much to refactor. However, in more complex scenarios, refactoring is crucial to improve code readability, maintainability, and performance without changing its external behavior. This step ensures that the code is clean and efficient after passing the tests."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Adding More Tests"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's add another test case to handle negative numbers:"},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"// sum.test.js
const sum = require('./sum');

describe('sum', () => {
  it('should add two numbers correctly', () => {
    expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3);
  });

  it('should handle negative numbers', () => {
    expect(sum(-1, 2)).toBe(1);
  });
});"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run `npm test` again to ensure all tests pass."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of TDD"},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved code quality: Writing tests first forces you to think about the requirements and design of your code."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced debugging time: Tests act as living documentation and help catch bugs early."},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Better design: TDD encourages modular and loosely coupled code."},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Confidence in code changes: Tests provide a safety net when refactoring or adding new features."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial provided a basic introduction to Test-Driven Development (TDD) in JavaScript. By writing tests before code, you can improve the quality, design, and maintainability of your applications. Remember to practice the red-green-refactor cycle to fully leverage the benefits of TDD."}]
